syntax:
template <class T, class Container = std::deque<T>> class std::stack;
Practical usage: std::stack<Type, UnderlyingContainer> stackName(initializer);
- T: The element type (e.g., std::string for string elements).
- UnderlyingContainer: The container type, such as std::vector<T> for dynamic arrays.
Notes: When initializing with another container, like std::stack<std::string, std::vector<std::string>> str_stk2(svec);, it invokes the copy constructor of the underlying container, creating an independent copy. Use std::move(svec) to avoid copies if svec is expendable, promoting efficiency in real-world code.

code_example:
#include <stack>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> svec = {"one", "two", "three"};
    std::stack<std::string, std::vector<std::string>> str_stk2(svec);  // Creates a copy of svec
    // Example usage: Push and pop elements
    str_stk2.push("four");  // Adds to the top
    if (!str_stk2.empty()) {
        std::cout << "Top element: " << str_stk2.top() << std::endl;  // Outputs the top element
        str_stk2.pop();  // Removes the top element
    }
    return 0;
}
